Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 22:19:10 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: SPACE:DC-X Flies

> Why does the DC-X need to hover, move horizontally, and land vertically? 
> This seems to  be rather extravagant behavior...

It's all part of SSTO's (single stage to orbit) rather extravagent 
intended mode of operation: launch directly to orbit, deploy payload, 
reenter, and land, all on rocket power with a single reusable stage.  

The payoff is that this makes the SSTO work more like an airliner 
than do contemporary rockets which chuck expensive rocket engines
and tanks overboard on the way up, that are either use-once or must be 
recovered and refurbished at great cost.

The drawback is that much more fuel, oxidizer, and tank is needed to
get the same payload to orbit, because the the engines have to lift
the complete tank all the way, plus fuel and oxidizer for the return trip.
With multi-stage rockets the heavy first stage tanks are discarded
when their propellant runs out.  Thus, the SSTO tanks must be extremely 
lightweight to get any payload into orbit at all; there's a big debate 
on whether it can be done with current materials.

Note that even if successful SSTO only gets to low orbit;
additional stages or refueling operations are needed to get to 
Clarke orbit or escape velocity.  (I favor native material supplied
stages; cf. my essay on comet mining).

