From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Durk and Sandy Do Cable

Dan Davis:
> Could you elaborate on any of their particular recommendations that
> you disagree with? 

Naked amino acids are a very promising area, but I'm pretty leary of taking
them with our current knowledge.  The basic idea
is to get a high amount of specific amino acid(s) in the bloodstream,
instead of the normal protein mix.  One major use is to
bias the brain's production of neurotransmitters by increasing the
concentration of their amino acids precursors.  For example, the amino 
acid tryptophan is a precursor to serotonin, so taking lots of tryptophan 
on an empty stomach has sleep-inducing and sometimes anti-depressant 
effects.  Taking arginine and/or ornithine causes the brain to release 
more human growth hormone, which can help bulk up muscles, cut down fat
accumulation and may slow down aging. (HGH goes way down after the
early 20s, and injecting HGH in old people reverses many aging symptoms).
Many "smart nutrients" are also based on amino acid and other 
nutrient precursors to neurotransmitters like acetylcholine, 
dopamine, etc.

Another use for naked amino acids is to increase precursors to natural 
antioxidants.  For example, cysteine is a bottleneck building block for 
(either glutathione or SOD, forget which right now) recommended by
both Durk & Sandy and Dr. Roy Walford.

The ability of naked amino acids to alter brain function is often
subtle, not very well studied, and can rub both ways.  For example,
I've seen articles in _Science_ that glutamine and cysteine can 
be highly neurotoxic under some conditions (eg embryo development -- 
which admittedly may be a quite unusual circumstance, since neuron death 
actually plays an important positive role in that process).
Call me paranoid, but on this slim evidence I've stopped taking
cysteine as part of my antioxidant mix.

I suspect (hope?) phenylalanine is safe unless you're phenylketonuric
(a rare highly specific genetic disease that appears in early
childhood), since it's widely used in aspartame (aspartic acid +
phenylalanine).  Millions of people, including me, get huge doses of
naked phenylalanine and aspartic acid every day in their Diet Cokes.

