Date: Mon, 20 Sep 93 23:18:00 -0700
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Being w/Smart People

I feel privileged to be around really smart people; I thirst for it, and
this is why I left Michigan and moved to Silicon Valley. 

"Smart" is hard to define; it has many nuances in everyday spoken
vernacular at least.  Various indicators are used to indicate being
"smart." My (too many) years as an academic taught me that "smart" is not
necessarily related to years of schooling, or mental 'facts on file' or top
grades, the loudest voice, the most (ob)noxious personality, or haranguing
interaction style. Academics who are driven to vociferously defend to
victory every point, no matter how trivial, often expose basic weaknesses.
When something really important comes up, a strategem of their defeat is
often evident and easily executed. As for smart, I remember the song by Ry
Cooder that says, "the farmer feeds us all." Living well, I suspect, has
something to do with being smart. So does making a contribution in one's
chosen arena of focus.

More to the point, I seek to be around people who are really *good* at
certain things. I've never met anyone who worked as hard as some musicians
and dancers I know: their combination of talent, creativity and virtuoso
technical skill are a pleasure to encounter. To see a college library
designed by an architect friend is a treat. Observing the diplomatic skills
of another friend who can take business adversaries at war and bring them
to acceptable conclusion has taught me that she reads human interaction and
dynamics on a plane beyond my ken. Of course, the bright minds of
cryptography, nanotechnology, biotech, VR, materials, object-oriented
development, law, ... , are also bright lights. I must also include some
(NOT ALL) of the venture capital folx I'm around. From all these different
perspectives, my mind is regularly filled with fresh ideas. It's a thrill.

But, at least in public settings, my personality tends to be more quiet: I
tend to take to heart the fortune cookie that told me "from listening comes
wisdom, from speaking repentance." I suspect this is from the many years of
leading seminars and conducting focus groups. Nevertheless, it's a pleasure
to be around the megawatt heads who spew their ideas. Thanks, everybody.
