>(see alt.philosophy.objectivism for the philosophical disproof of
>quantum theory, or the objectivism list where homosexuality was proven
>objectively immoral)

Didn't follow the quantum theory debate, but homosexuality was never
proven immoral on the list I read.  Two guys kept trying to do so,
but they got blown out of the water every time.  Both relied upon unsound
premises in their attempts.

>  Really, there is no such thing as engineeritis, it's a bogus
>category invented by those who wish to automatically discredit an
>argument by ad hominem.

This, in turn, is an ad hominem, circumstantial.

I assure you, Ray, I have no such desire, nor do I know of any proof that
Hayek did, either.  Nor was it invented, but discovered.  I coined the
term to refer to things that actually exist, real phenomena.  Nor did
I include any negative evaluation in the denotation, although I admit
that the "itis" part gives it some negative connotation.

Surprisingly, no one has tried either of the two legitimate response
strategies: defense of the phenomena, or denial of it (although denying
the evidence isn't always legitmate, either).  Some could have argued
that the methods of physics are universally applicable, some could have
argued that no one acts like they are.

Instead, the only response has been to complain about the naming of the
phenomena.  Feel free to rename it, for all I care!  We have no linguistic
legal tender laws - you aren't forced to accept my coinage.

However, if the true fundamental objection is to discussion of this
phenomena at all, then too bad.  Either exclude it, or sue for violation
of list rules if you think you can prove your case.

>There is a tendency of people on this list to
>analyze political systems with mathematical models (with economic
>models, game theory, etc) sure, but only because those are much better
>ways of figuring out what happens.

I never objected to this.  I objected to the notion that nothing else
matters when it comes to method.

As for their being better, than what?  I'd say that they're better than
some methods (e.g., entrails-reading), but possibly worse than others.

